WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.588
So what does the example add?

2
00:00:01.588 --> 00:00:04.610
One configuration, which
audits that old protocol

3
00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.562
versions are explicitly
switched off on servers.

4
00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:12.343
It's audit only, and a server with no
explicit setting counts as non-compliant,

5
00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.137
on purpose, because the baseline
wants the setting on record.

6
00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:20.337
A module of checks, and a build script
that runs the whole thing offline.

7
00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:22.678
The heart of it is the release registry:

8
00:00:22.678 --> 00:00:25.312
the reviewed record of
what's in production.

9
00:00:25.312 --> 00:00:29.952
It fixes the policy identifier forever,
the mode, the published version,

10
00:00:29.952 --> 00:00:34.250
the identity, and the owner tag that
keeps other tools' servers out.
